    {
      "title": "RNNs, Vanishing Gradients, LSTM and GRU",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 3,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "RNNs, Vanishing Gradients, LSTM and GRU",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. The RNN and Its Gradient",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`h_t = tanh(W_h*h_{t-1} + W_x*x_t + b)`",
                "==Same weights== at every time step",
                "Hidden state ==h== carries memory of the sequence",
                "Training = ==BPTT==: gradient is a product of many **Jacobians**"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Factors",
                "Gradient",
                "Effect"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "{red:< 1}",
                  "==vanishes==",
                  "can't learn long-range dependencies"
                ],
                [
                  "{orange:> 1}",
                  "==explodes==",
                  "unstable training"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Fixes and Gated Cells",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Exploding: ==gradient clipping==",
                "Vanishing: ==gated cells== (LSTM/GRU) and good initialization",
                "LSTM: cell state + {blue:forget}, {green:input}, {orange:output} gates",
                "`c_t = f_t*c_{t-1} + i_t*g_t` is ==additive==, so gradients survive long spans"
              ],
              "style": "check",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "LSTM",
                "GRU"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "State",
                  "cell + hidden",
                  "merged"
                ],
                [
                  "Gates",
                  "3 (forget, input, output)",
                  "2 (update, reset)"
                ],
                [
                  "Cost",
                  "more parameters",
                  "fewer, faster"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Accuracy is similar on many tasks"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Common Mistakes",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Forgetting `batch_first` and the (batch, time, features) shape",
                "Padding without masking"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "Additive cell update = ==gradient highway==. Clip for explosions, gate for vanishing."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Why Transformers Replaced RNNs",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 4,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Why Transformers Replaced RNNs",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Parallel and Short Paths",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "RNN: ==one token at a time==, so no parallel training over the sequence",
                "Transformer: {green:all tokens at once}, a good fit for GPUs",
                "Self-attention links any two positions in ==one step==",
                "Long-range signal does not fade through repeated state updates"
              ],
              "style": "check"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "RNN",
                "Transformer"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Path length",
                  "O(n)",
                  "==O(1)=="
                ],
                [
                  "Training",
                  "sequential",
                  "parallel"
                ],
                [
                  "Memory/compute",
                  "O(n)",
                  "O(n^2)"
                ],
                [
                  "Inference state",
                  "constant size",
                  "grows with n"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Attention",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "python",
              "lines": [
                "out = softmax(Q @ K.T / sqrt(d_k)) @ V"
              ],
              "note": "dividing by sqrt(d_k) keeps scores stable"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "{orange:Positional encodings} are required: attention has ==no notion of order=="
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Trade-offs and Mistakes",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Self-attention is ==O(n^2)== in memory and compute; an RNN is O(n)",
                "RNNs stay cheaper for very long **streaming inference**"
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Assuming CNNs and RNNs are obsolete",
                "CNNs still dominate efficient vision and edge models",
                "State-space and linear-recurrent models revisit recurrence for long sequences"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "Transformers trade ==O(n^2)== cost for parallel training and O(1) paths."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
